:3 unshown, T5:1 \u2192 possible\nSo only possible chow with T3 is [T3,T4,T5]\n\nAny player building that? Next player discarded T4, T5 \u2014 so unlikely\nAcross player discarded T5, T6, T9 \u2014 unclear\nPrevious player hasn\u2019t shown T\n\nStill, T3 has some residual risk\n\nBut W7: **zero risk** \u2014 no possible chow can be made with it\n\nTherefore, **Play W7** is safer\n\nMoreover, keeping T3 gives a slightly better chance for future flexibility? Not really \u2014 but pairing with T3 is fine.\n\nBut scoring: if I eventually win with pair of T3 vs W7 \u2014 no difference.\n\nHowever, **T3 is a lower tile**, and if I ever aim for Lower Tiles or similar, but no \u2014 I have B7, T6 \u2014 high tiles.\n\nIrrelevant.\n\nFinal decision: **Discard W7** \u2014 it is safer due to W6 and W8 being extinct, making it impossible to complete any chow containing W7.\n\nAnswer must be one of: [Play W7; Play T3]\n\nAnswer: Play W7\n\n---\n\nSituation: I have four melds and two single tiles (W7, T3), needing to discard one to maintain a legal hand. The [W6, W7, W8] chow is already formed, and W6/W8 are fully depleted from the wall, making W7 unusable in any chow for other players.\n\nEvaluation: Discarding W7 is extremely safe because no player can use it to complete a chow, as W6 and W8 are already exhausted. Discarding T3 carries slight risk as it could complete a [T3,T4,T5] chow, though evidence suggests minimal exposure.\n\nReason: W7 is a safe tile due to the extinction of its neighboring tiles (W6 and W8), eliminating any chow threat. Preserving T3 avoids unnecessary risk and maintains hand balance.\n\nAnswer: Play W7"}
